The Biographies of Zhang Shizhi and Feng Tang

Zhang Tingwei Shizhi was a native of Duyang, and his style name was Ji. He had an elder brother named Zhong with whom he lived together. He was appointed a riding attendant on the strength of his family's property, served Emperor Xiaowen, and over the course of ten years was never promoted and became known for nothing. Shizhi said, "Having served so long has depleted my brother's property, and I have not succeeded." He wished to resign and return home. The commander of the central division, Yuan Ang, recognized his worth and regretted his departure, so he requested that Shizhi be transferred to fill the post of attendant. After Shizhi had finished paying his court call, he took the opportunity to speak on matters of immediate concern. The emperor said, "Keep it simple; do not raise matters too high; make them something that can be put into practice today." Then Shizhi spoke at length on the affairs between the Qin and the Han, on why Qin lost and why Han prevailed. The emperor praised him, and appointed Shizhi as the head of attendants.

Shizhi accompanied the emperor on a journey and climbed into the tiger enclosure. The emperor asked the superintendent of the imperial hunting park about the registers of the birds and beasts, asking more than ten questions. The superintendent glanced around on both sides and could not answer a single one. The keeper of the tiger enclosure answered from the side, in place of the superintendent, on all the questions the emperor asked about the registers of birds and beasts, very completely, wishing to observe whether he could answer extemporaneously and respond endlessly. The emperor said, "Should an official be like this? The superintendent is a scoundrel!" So he issued an edict appointing Shizhi to confer the office of superintendent of the imperial hunting park upon the keeper. Later Shizhi approached him and said, "Your Majesty, how would you rate Zhou Bo of Jianghou?" The emperor said, "A man of integrity." Then he asked again, "How would you rate Zhang Xiang of Dongyang?" The emperor again said, "A man of integrity." Shizhi said, "Zhou Bo of Jianghou and Zhang Xiang of Dongyang are both called men of integrity, yet when they speak on matters they cannot even bring words out of their mouths. How could they imitate this keeper, with his babbling, his sharp tongue, his quick and clever replies! Moreover, Qin relied on clerks who worked with the brush and the seal; officials competed with one another in being quick, severe, and exacting. Yet its end was merely formalities, with no genuine compassion at all. For this reason its faults were never heard of, and it declined until the reign of the second emperor, when the whole world collapsed like a landslide. Now Your Majesty, on the strength of this keeper's eloquence, has promoted him beyond his rank. I fear that the whole world will follow the wind and the dust, contending in eloquence while lacking substance. Moreover, the influence of the lower ranks upon the higher is as swift as the shadow and the echo; therefore one's measures and actions cannot fail to be carefully considered." The emperor said, "Good." So he stopped and did not confer the office upon the keeper.

The emperor went up to his carriage and summoned Shizhi to ride beside him. They drove slowly, and the emperor asked Shizhi about the decline of Qin. Shizhi answered him in full and in plain terms. On arriving at the palace, the emperor conferred upon Shizhi the office of commissioner of the imperial carriage.

Not long afterward, the crown prince and the king of Liang rode in the same carriage into court and did not dismount at the gate of the office of the commander-in-chief. Shizhi then pursued and stopped them, saying that they must not enter the palace gate. He brought charges against them for insolence in not dismounting at the public gate, and submitted the case. When Lady Bo, the emperor's mother, heard of it, Emperor Wen took off his cap and bowed in apology, saying, "I have failed to teach my sons properly." Lady Bo then sent an envoy to convey an edict pardoning the crown prince and the king of Liang, and only then were they allowed to enter. From this time the emperor regarded Shizhi as remarkable and appointed him a senior attendant.

Not long afterward he was raised to the rank of commander of the central division. He accompanied the emperor on a journey to Bailang, where they stayed at the northern latrine. At that time Shen Furen was in attendance, and the emperor pointed to the new road to Xinfeng and said to Shen Furen, "This is the road to Handan." He had Shen Furen play the zither, and he himself leaned on the zither and sang. His mood was sad and his heart sorrowful, so he turned to his ministers and said, "Alas! If I could use stone from the Northern Mountains for coffins, and stuff them with hemp and flax, and seal the seams with lacquer, how could they be moved!" Those around him all said, "Good." Shizhi stepped forward and said, "If there were anything within that could be desired, even if the mountains of the South were encased in stone there would still be a gap; if there were nothing within that could be desired, even without a stone coffin, what sorrow would there be!" The emperor praised him. Later Shizhi was appointed Tingwei.

Not long afterward the emperor went out through the middle bridge over the Wei River. A man came out from beneath the bridge and seized a horse of the imperial carriage, which was startled. The emperor then sent cavalry to capture him and handed him over to the Tingwei. Shizhi investigated the case. The man said, "I am a native of the county. I heard the imperial signal and hid beneath the bridge. For a long time I thought the procession had already passed, so I came out. I saw the imperial carriage and its escort, and so I ran." The Tingwei applied the law of Qin: one who violated the imperial signal was to be fined. The emperor was angry and said, "This man actually startled my horse. My horse happened to be gentle; had it been another horse, would it not have trampled and injured me? Yet the Tingwei has sentenced him only to a fine!" Shizhi said, "The law is what the sovereign shares with the whole world. Now the law is as it is, and if we make it heavier, then the law will not be trusted by the people. Moreover, at that moment, if Your Majesty had ordered him executed on the spot, that would have been the end of it. Now that he has been handed down to the Tingwei, the Tingwei is the standard of justice throughout the world; if it is once tilted, then the application of the law everywhere will be made light or heavy at will, and where will the people know how to place their hands and feet? Only Your Majesty can examine this." After a long time the emperor said, "The Tingwei's judgment is correct."

Later someone stole a jade ring from before the throne of the High Ancestor's temple. He was captured, and the emperor was angry and handed him down to the Tingwei for trial. Shizhi examined the law and reported that a thief of the vestments and articles of the ancestral temple should be sentenced to death by exposure. The emperor was greatly enraged and said, "A man without principle has gone so far as to steal the vessels of the temple of the former emperor. I handed him down to the Tingwei in order to have him destroyed by the law of the clan, but you have brought him to trial under the law. That is not the way in which I wish to honor and carry forward the ancestral temple." Shizhi took off his cap and bowed his head to the ground in apology, saying, "The law is sufficient as it is. Moreover, the crimes are equal, but there is a difference between rebellion and obedience. Now, if a thief of the vessels of the ancestral temple is destroyed by the law of the clan, there is a chance in ten thousand that it will happen. Suppose a foolish commoner were to take a handful of earth from the tomb of the late emperor of Changling; what law could Your Majesty add to punish him?" After a long time the emperor spoke of the matter with the empress dowager, and then he allowed the Tingwei's judgment. At that time the commander of the capital, Zhou Yafu of the marquisate of Tiaohou, and Wang Tiankai, the governor-general of Liang, saw that Shizhi held his opinions with fairness, and so they became intimate friends. From this time the Tingwei Zhang was praised throughout the world.

Afterward Emperor Wen died, and Emperor Jing ascended the throne. Shizhi was afraid and claimed illness. He wished to be relieved of office, but feared that a great punishment would come; he wished to go and make his apology, but did not know how it would go. Following the plan of Wang Sheng, he finally went to make his apology, and Emperor Jing did not go beyond that.

Wang Sheng was a man skilled in the words of the Yellow Emperor and Laozi, and a retired scholar. He was once summoned and stood in the court, when the three dukes and the nine ministers all stood assembled. Wang Sheng, an old man, said, "My sandal strap has come loose," and turning to Tingwei Zhang he said, "Tie my sandal strap for me!" Shizhi knelt and tied it. When it was done, someone said to Wang Sheng, "Why did you alone humiliate Tingwei Zhang, making him kneel to tie your sandal strap?" Wang Sheng said, "I am old and lowly, and I judged that in the end it would be of no benefit to Tingwei Zhang. Tingwei Zhang is today a famous minister of the world; so I deliberately humiliated Tingwei, made him kneel to tie my sandal strap, in order to raise his standing." When the public officials heard this, they praised Wang Sheng and held Tingwei Zhang in high regard.

Tingwei Zhang served Emperor Jing for a year or so, and then became the governor-general of Huainan, still being held back on account of his former offense. After a long time Shizhi died. His son was Zhang Zhi, style name Changgong, who held office as high as the rank of senior attendant, and then was relieved. Because he could not accommodate himself to the men of his time, he never again took office.

Feng Tang was a descendant of a family of the state of Zhao. His father moved to Dai. When the Han was founded, the family moved to Anling. Tang was noted for his filial conduct, and served as the head of the central division, attending Emperor Wen. When the emperor passed in his carriage, he asked Tang, "Old man, how did you come to be an official? Where is your home?" Tang answered him in full and truthfully. The emperor said, "When I was in Dai, my supervisor of the food, Gao Qu, often spoke to me of the worth of the Zhao general Li Qi, and of his victory beneath Julu. Now at every meal my thoughts are never away from Julu. Do you know him?" Tang answered, "He is not even as good as the generals Lian Po and Li Mu." The emperor said, "How do you know?" Tang said, "When my grandfather was in Zhao he served as a military commander and was on good terms with Li Mu. My father formerly served as the governor of Dai and was on good terms with the Zhao general Li Qi, and so knew him as a man." After the emperor had heard of the character of Lian Po and Li Mu, he was greatly pleased, and, slapping his thigh, he said, "Alas! I alone could not have Lian Po and Li Mu as my generals at that time. If I had, how could I have worried about the Xiongnu!" Tang said, "Sir, even if Your Majesty obtained Lian Po and Li Mu, you would not be able to use them." The emperor was angry, and rose and went into the forbidden palace. After a long time he summoned Tang and rebuked him, saying, "Why did you publicly humiliate me, when you had no private place to do it?" Tang apologized, saying, "I am a vulgar person who does not know the proprieties."

At that time the Xiongnu had newly made a great incursion into Chaona and killed the prefect of Beidi, Ang. The emperor took the Huns as his concern, and so finally asked Tang again, "How do you know that I cannot use Lian Po and Li Mu?" Tang answered, "I have heard that when the ancient kings sent out their generals, they knelt and pushed the shaft of the chariot, saying, 'Within the gate it is I who decide; without the gate it is the general who decides. Military merit, rank, and reward are all decided outside, and reported upon return.' This is not empty talk. My grandfather said that Li Mu, as general of Zhao, stationed at the frontier, made the tax of the military market entirely for his own use in entertaining his soldiers, and that rewards and punishments were decided outside, without interference from the center. By entrusting him with authority and demanding only success, Li Mu was able to bring forth all his intelligence and ability. He dispatched a thousand and three hundred chariots, thirteen thousand horsemen, and a hundred thousand men of the gold class, and so drove the Xiongnu back to the north, broke the Donghu, destroyed the Danlin, held back the strong Qin to the west, and supported Han and Wei to the south. At that time Zhao was almost a hegemon. Afterward, by chance, the king of Zhao, Qian, was placed on the throne, and his mother was a singer. When Qian was placed on the throne, he followed the calumny of Guo Kai and finally put Li Mu to death, ordering Yan Ju to take his place. For this reason his army was broken and his soldiers fled, and he was captured and destroyed by Qin. Now I have privately heard that Wei Shang is the governor of Yunzhong, and that the tax of his military market is entirely given to entertain his soldiers, and that he spends his own money to keep cattle, slaughtering one every five days, and entertains his guests, the military officers, and the household attendants. For this reason the Xiongnu kept far away and did not approach the frontier of Yunzhong. When a bandit once came in, Wei Shang led his chariots and cavalry to attack him, and the number of those he killed was great. The soldiers are all the sons of common families, who rose from the fields to enlist; how can they know the measures of the army and the marks of the ranks? They fight all day, beheading and capturing bandits, and report their merits to the military governor's office. If one word does not correspond, the clerks bind them by the law. Their rewards are not carried out, while the clerks enforce the law and must do so. I am foolish, and I think that Your Majesty's law is too clear, your rewards too meager, and your punishments too heavy. Moreover, Wei Shang, the governor of Yunzhong, was convicted of reporting the number of bandits' heads cut off with a difference of six grades, and Your Majesty handed him down to the clerks, stripped him of his rank, and sentenced him to labor. From this it follows that even if Your Majesty obtained Lian Po and Li Mu, you would not be able to use them. I am truly foolish, and I have touched upon a taboo. A death crime, a death crime!" The emperor was pleased. On that day he ordered Feng Tang to carry the insignia and pardon Wei Shang, restoring him to the office of governor of Yunzhong, and appointed Tang as commander of the chariot cavalry, in charge of the central division and the chariot troops of the commanderies and kingdoms.

In the seventh year Emperor Jing ascended the throne, and Tang was made the governor of Chu, and then relieved. When Emperor Wu ascended the throne, he sought men of worth and virtue, and nominated Feng Tang. Tang was then more than ninety years old and could no longer hold office, so his son Feng Sui was made an attendant. Sui's style name was Wangsun, and he was also a remarkable man, and was a friend of mine.

The Grand Historian says: The words of Zhang Ji are those of a man of integrity; he holds the law without bowing to the will. The discourse of Feng Gong on the selection of generals is truly wonderful! Truly wonderful! The saying is, "He who does not know a man, look at his friends." The two men whom these are praised for may be recorded in the halls of the court. The Book says, "Neither partial nor factional, the way of the king is broad; neither factional nor partial, the way of the king is smooth." Zhang Ji and Feng Gong come near to this. [end of text]


